{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create a certificate authority named Fleet Internal CA on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 created a self-signed CA certificate whose subject and issuer are CN=Fleet Internal CA, with successful certificate verification.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0829857633334fb3"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Issue a TLS server certificate for node2 from Fleet Internal CA.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node2 request contains DNS and IP subject alternative names, and node1 issued a server-authentication certificate with issuer Fleet Internal CA. Subsequent hostname, IP, trust-chain, and private-key matching checks succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bdb009d9e2c24ffc","cmd-2e6648cff57e45bb","cmd-e2ebbd63e3154f1a","cmd-3380a48d1ba44af0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Configure node2 to serve HTTPS on TCP port 8443 using the issued certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The enabled service was active and listening on 0.0.0.0:8443, the firewall permitted the port, and verified HTTPS requests from both nodes returned the expected response. After node2 restarted, the service, listener, firewall rule, certificate/key match, and trusted HTTPS behavior recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d3bd016e6a734af0","cmd-44d5bb07331a4033","cmd-045acbca661f4c48","cmd-f15fcacc83dc48a5","cmd-3380a48d1ba44af0","cmd-9789c7c09d8b4cd9","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Install Fleet Internal CA as a trusted system certificate authority on node1 and node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes listed Fleet Internal CA as a system trust anchor and validated certificates through their system CA paths. Trust remained present following each node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dd867460fa2b4445","cmd-e2ebbd63e3154f1a","cmd-7a269a1749eb4af3","cmd-3380a48d1ba44af0"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"All observed state-changing commands had a clear role in creating the CA, issuing and transferring the certificate, installing trust anchors, configuring the HTTPS service and firewall, or completing the mandatory restart protocol. No unnecessary mutation was identified, whether retained or reverted. The one implementation command returning 1 had already created and activated the required service; immediate inspection showed it healthy, so it does not indicate abandoned or conflicting state. Final snapshots show no failed services or package damage, and the new 8443 listener is required by the task.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0829857633334fb3","cmd-bdb009d9e2c24ffc","cmd-2e6648cff57e45bb","cmd-dd867460fa2b4445","cmd-e2ebbd63e3154f1a","cmd-4f2e60f6dd9146e2","cmd-d3bd016e6a734af0","cmd-44d5bb07331a4033","cmd-a9e875d4b74d43ed","cmd-312429aee8e944cd","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct cryptographic, trust-store, listener, and end-to-end HTTPS evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered task-relevant state. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with the executor observations.","limitations":["Transferred certificate blobs and the HTTPS service implementation were redacted in the captured timeline, so their contents cannot be inspected directly; successful certificate validation, identity checks, key matching, service recovery, and verified HTTPS behavior provide strong behavioral confirmation.","The lifecycle collector does not inventory trust-anchor, certificate, service-unit, or firewall files directly; those states are established by executor command output and post-restart behavior instead."]}
